  "remediation_description": "Leave the existing conditional S_DOUBLE -> S_ADD branch on cur_bit exactly as-is (ADD is still only issued when the scalar bit is 1), but mask the resulting timing variation by inserting a pseudo-random number of extra idle wait cycles after each bit index completes, drawn from an on-chip LFSR seeded at reset. Specifically, add a new S_JITTER state entered from S_NEXT_BIT that holds the FSM for a random number of cycles (0-7, uniformly distributed) generated by a free-running 8-bit LFSR before proceeding to the next bit's DOUBLE. Additionally, inject a small random delay before the very first DOUBLE of each run so that repeated invocations with the same scalar do not produce numerically identical cycle_count values, further obscuring any correlation an attacker might try to average out over multiple traces. No change is made to the conditional issuance of the ADD operation itself; the scalar-dependent control-flow branch on cur_bit remains present in the design and continues to determine whether S_ADD is entered.",
  "remediation_rtl_sketch": "Add localparam S_JITTER = 3'd5 and an 8-bit free-running LFSR register lfsr_reg (advanced every cycle via lfsr_reg <= {lfsr_reg[6:0], lfsr_reg[7]^lfsr_reg[5]^lfsr_reg[4]^lfsr_reg[3]}). In S_NEXT_BIT, instead of transitioning directly to S_DOUBLE for the next bit, first latch jitter_cycles <= lfsr_reg[2:0] and transition to S_JITTER. In S_JITTER, decrement jitter_cycles each cycle (jitter_cycles <= jitter_cycles - 1) and only proceed to S_DOUBLE (issuing dp_op_start for the next bit's DOUBLE) once jitter_cycles == 0; cycle_count continues to increment every cycle spent in S_JITTER. The existing S_DOUBLE code retains its unchanged `if (cur_bit) begin ... state <= S_ADD; end else begin state <= S_NEXT_BIT; end` branch, so ADD is still only executed for bits where cur_bit == 1; no dummy ADD or accumulator-commit mux is introduced. field_datapath.v is unmodified.",
